          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Daube is one of the oldest dishes in Provence. It takes its name from the daubierre, a tall earthenware pot used for slow cooking. The dish is often made a day ahead because the flavour improves overnight. Markets in Nice and Marseille sell ready-made daube in winter. It is sometimes served over fresh pasta called pappardelle."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
